>
> This reply has research and questions only, so there is no spec and no
> patch. The lab-mail thread set that order. You asked for questions
> first [1], then for no open questions in a spec [2], then for "reply
> with an updated patch ... or reply with questions, but not both" [3].
> Once you answer the questions below, I will post a spec for beastie's
> client. It will carry an alternatives section, and the implementation
> follows after you approve it.
>
> Each claim below is marked [fact] (checked in the tree or on the list)
> or [inference] (my reasoning, not verified).
>
> Where things stand today:
>
> - [fact] lab-mail is not on trunk yet. The latest series is "[PATCH v11
>   0/8] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the mail relay jail" [4]. Its
>   spec puts your client out of scope.
> - [fact] smtprelay listens on 127.0.0.1:2525 with allowed_nets
>   127.0.0.0/8 and no client auth. allowed_sender matches only
>   claude@p7x.dev
>   (jails/instances/lab-mail/dist/usr/local/etc/smtprelay/smtprelay.ini
>   lines 12-19).
> - [fact] Dovecot serves the "lists" mirror read-only on
>   127.0.0.1:2143. It accepts any user name and password.
> - [fact] Emacs on beastie sends through libexec/maildir-deliver.sh,
>   which drops the message into the jj maildir (dotfiles/emacs/init.el
>   line 575). Nothing goes over SMTP.
> - [fact] infra/hosts/beastie/ports lists notmuch, notmuch-emacs, and
>   password-store, but not msmtp or isync.
>
>> One question I have is: how do I keep my mail credentials secure? If
>> it's pat@patmaddox.com - well that's my main mail account. How do other
>> mail clients keep credentials secure? I guess they must just save them
>> somewhere?
>
> If beastie sends through lab-mail, beastie holds no credential at all.
> [fact] The one Fastmail app password lives in
> /jails/data/lab-mail/secrets/mail-creds on the host. It is mounted
> read-only into lab-mail and readable only by group mailcred. [fact]
> smtprelay takes unauthenticated SMTP on loopback. So Emacs would call
> msmtp with "auth off" against 127.0.0.1:2525, exactly as lab-claude's
> .msmtprc already does, and there is no password to store.
>
> To your general question: yes, clients store the secret somewhere, and
> the common setups differ in where and in how much the secret can do.
> [inference, from the clients' documentation as I know it; I have not
> tested these here]
>
> - An app password rather than the account password. Fastmail and Gmail
>   both issue them. One app password can be revoked alone, and on
>   Fastmail it can be limited to SMTP. This matters more than where it
>   is stored.
> - A password manager the client calls when it sends. msmtp has
>   "passwordeval", e.g. "pass show mail/fastmail-smtp", and
>   git-send-email takes a credential helper. Emacs reads
>   ~/.authinfo.gpg through auth-source. The secret rests encrypted
>   under GPG or the OS keychain, and it is decrypted only at send time.
> - A local MTA with a smarthost, such as dma, OpenSMTPD, or postfix,
>   with the credential in a root-only file. Clients send to localhost
>   with no auth. This is the lab-mail design.
>
>> Perhaps I use the mail relay for notmuch and do pat@p7x.dev as the
>> outgoing? That way I don't have to use my main account.
>
> The relay is a good fit, and it holds the credential, so your client
> holds none whichever address you send from. Two things to settle first.
>
> - [fact] pat@p7x.dev is not an allowed sender, so it needs a line in
>   allowed_sender. [inference] It also needs a Fastmail sending
>   identity on the account behind mail-creds, as claude@p7x.dev has.
>   Otherwise Fastmail may reject the From or rewrite it.
> - [inference] The relay trusts all of 127.0.0.0/8, and every
>   ip4=inherit jail on beastie shares that loopback, lab-claude among
>   them. Once pat@p7x.dev is allowed, I could send mail as you from
>   lab-claude, and nothing would stop me. Today that holds only for
>   claude@p7x.dev. Fixing it means separate client credentials on the
>   relay. I believe smtprelay's allowed_users file can tie each user to
>   the sender addresses they may use, but I have not checked it.
>   Another fix gives lab-claude its own IP, which you said would come
>   later.
>
>> Though - tons of developers use notmuch or something similar for sending
>> emails to the mailing list. Research to find out how they configure it.
>
> [inference, from the projects' docs and common practice] The usual
> kernel and git contributor setup has three parts.
>
> - Reading: a local maildir indexed by notmuch, filled by mbsync or
>   offlineimap from IMAP, or by lei/b4 straight from lore. notmuch never
>   talks to the network itself.
> - Sending: notmuch-emacs uses message-mode with
>   message-send-mail-with-sendmail and sendmail-program set to msmtp.
>   Patches go through git send-email, which can use the same msmtp
>   account. msmtp's password comes from passwordeval or a 0600 file.
> - No credential at all: b4's web submission endpoint takes patches
>   signed with a patatt key, so no SMTP password is needed. Our relay
>   gets the same result with less work.
>
> Your plan matches that pattern, with lab-mail as both the IMAP source
> and the smarthost. This is the plan I would spec.
>
> 1. beastie installs mail/msmtp and mail/isync. These are new
>    dependencies for infra/hosts/beastie/ports.
> 2. mbsync pulls "lists" from 127.0.0.1:2143 into a local maildir. A
>    notmuch profile indexes it, as lab-claude does.
> 3. Emacs sends through msmtp to 127.0.0.1:2525 with no auth, from
>    whichever address you choose below.
> 4. smtprelay allows that address as a sender, after we settle the
>    loopback trust issue.
>
> Questions before I write the spec:
>
> a) Reading: should Emacs move off the jj maildir for lab-devel? Or
>    should the IMAP mirror sit beside it as a second notmuch profile? The
>    patch workflow, process-email included, reads the jj maildir. If
>    you move, your mail stops reaching the list through jj, so the
>    moderated-delivery step in zib/patch-review-workflow.org changes.

Yes this will switch from the jj maildir to synced maildir. I think it
would be fine as a second profile, at least to start. Here's how I'd
generally like the work to go (where each phase may have multiple
